WEBVTT

00:00.775 --> 00:02.075
Let me show you something.

00:02.125 --> 00:03.537
This is a live engine.

00:03.587 --> 00:07.187
Every point is a word it knows, and every line is a relationship it worked

00:07.237 --> 00:10.025
out for itself. Nobody drew this.

00:11.984 --> 00:16.409
Here's Neuron. I'll ask it to define a word it has never been given.

00:17.318 --> 00:21.855
Watch the stage. It goes and reads, finds the word's relationships, and works them into

00:21.905 --> 00:25.268
the graph, along with everything it met on the way.

00:26.152 --> 00:29.265
That one line set off a whole tree of runs underneath it.

00:29.315 --> 00:33.602
Every run leaves a receipt, so let's open this one and play it back.

00:34.486 --> 00:37.211
This is the path it took through the graph, hop by hop.

00:37.261 --> 00:40.261
Not a summary of it. The actual path.

00:44.495 --> 00:46.308
Now I'll ask the same thing again.

00:46.358 --> 00:48.107
Nothing runs. It already knows.

00:48.157 --> 00:49.383
Learning happens once.

00:49.433 --> 00:51.370
Recall is instant.

00:52.212 --> 00:53.525
And this is Cortex.

00:53.575 --> 00:55.237
Same engine, different job.

00:55.287 --> 00:57.462
I tell it about a project in plain lines:

00:57.512 --> 01:00.400
what it is, where the checkpoints are, how things stand.

01:00.450 --> 01:03.712
Each line lands with its own time, so the next session starts from the

01:03.762 --> 01:06.662
state of the work, not from a transcript of it.

01:11.637 --> 01:13.012
Cortex and Neuron.

01:13.062 --> 01:15.500
Research and engineering by James Huson.

01:15.550 --> 01:17.937
Both at exnulla dot com.
